------------------------------------------------------------------------
crates/ddai-tsworld
------------------------------------------------------------------------

`crates/ddai-tsworld` is a literal Rust port of `tools/ts-reference/src/core/*.ts`
(`vmath.ts`, `tuning.ts`, `types.ts`, `collision.ts`, `characterCore.ts`,
`projectile.ts`, `world.ts`) and `tools/ts-reference/src/map/loadMap.ts` — the same TypeScript
files the "Third-party code" section above already covers, ported one more
step (TypeScript to Rust) instead of C++ to TypeScript. It exists only to
prove a future Rust port of the old planner (`tools/ts-reference/src/plan/*.ts`) makes the
same decisions the TS planner did on the TS physics it was tuned against
(decision D-035, `docs/DECISIONS.md`); it is not the project's production
physics (`crates/ddai-physics`, bit-exact with the real DDNet C++ server
instead). The same zlib-style DDNet notice above applies to this crate's
ported files (see each file's own header doc comment for the exact
`src/core/*.ts` file:line it mirrors, relative to `tools/ts-reference/`); the crate itself is licensed
GPL-3.0-only like the rest of this repository, with credit to the original
TS project (Wranked1/DDNet-AI) in its own README.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
crates/ddai-planner
------------------------------------------------------------------------

`crates/ddai-planner` is a literal Rust port of the old block planner
(`tools/ts-reference/src/plan/{planner,shield,seal,throwLines,memory,livePlan}.ts`,
the BFS fields in `planner.ts`, and the scripted bot / opponent models in
`tools/ts-reference/src/env/{scripted,action}.ts`) — the same TypeScript project the sections
above already cover (Wranked1/DDNet-AI, GPL-3.0). It proves the port makes
the exact same decisions the TS planner did (decision D-035), and, on
`ddai-physics::World<f32>`, is the precise-search half of the live bot's
decision-making (decision D-041). Licensed GPL-3.0-only like the rest of
this repository, with credit to the original TS project (Wranked1/DDNet-AI)
in its own README.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
crates/ddai-libm: glibc 2.39 math functions
------------------------------------------------------------------------

`crates/ddai-libm` re-implements, in Rust, the math functions of the GNU C
Library 2.39 (Ubuntu 24.04, x86-64 FMA variant) that DDNet 20.1's physics
calls on Linux, so that Linux and Windows compute identical bits (decisions
D-047, D-127). The code was ported from glibc's own source files (read from a
glibc 2.39 source tree; `tools/libm-tables/gen_tables.py` generates
`crates/ddai-libm/src/tables.rs` from the same tree), not from the upstream
projects glibc took some of it from. The source of each file is stated in its
header. No file was copied from a source that is not GPL-3.0-compatible. The
parts and their licences:

* `sincosf.rs` (`sinf`, `cosf`), `powf.rs`, `log.rs`, `pow.rs` and the tables
  of `tables.rs` they use: ported from glibc 2.39's
  `sysdeps/ieee754/flt-32/{s_sincosf.c,s_sincosf.h,s_sincosf_data.c,e_powf.c,
  e_powf_log2_data.c,e_exp2f_data.c}` and `sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/{e_log.c,
  e_log_data.c,e_pow.c,e_pow_log_data.c,e_exp_data.c}`. These files are
  Copyright (C) 2017-2024 (powf, exp2f tables) and 2018-2024 (the others) Free
  Software Foundation, Inc., licensed LGPL-2.1-or-later. Their algorithms and
  tables are the work of ARM Limited's optimized-routines
  (https://github.com/ARM-software/optimized-routines, `math/sincosf.c`,
  `powf.c`, `log.c`, `pow.c` and their data files; Copyright (c) 2017-2018,
  Arm Limited), which glibc imported in versions 2.27 and 2.28. Upstream was
  under Apache-2.0 for its first revisions (spring 2018), under MIT from
  2018-11-12 and under "MIT OR Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception" since
  2022-02-10; every revision is GPL-3.0-compatible, and the 5.5a review found
  every numeric table value of glibc 2.39 also in current upstream. Because
  the ports were made from glibc's copies, they carry glibc's licence (the
  LGPL, converted to the GPL below), with the Arm origin recorded in each
  header; the MIT text of the upstream is not what they are distributed under.

* `atanf.rs`, `atan2f.rs`: Sun Microsystems' fdlibm (`s_atanf.c`,
  `e_atan2f.c` as shipped in glibc), under the Sun notice, preserved in each
  header:

    Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc. All rights reserved.
    Developed at SunPro, a Sun Microsystems, Inc. business.
    Permission to use, copy, modify, and distribute this software is freely
    granted, provided that this notice is preserved.

* `atan2.rs` (double-precision `atan2`, with the table of `uatan.tbl` and the
  constants of `atnat2.h`): IBM Accurate Mathematical Library, Copyright (C)
  2001-2024 Free Software Foundation, Inc., licensed LGPL-2.1-or-later;
  `hypot.rs` (`hypotf`, `hypot`): Copyright (C) 2012-2024 (`hypotf`) and
  2021-2024 (`hypot`) Free Software Foundation, Inc., licensed
  LGPL-2.1-or-later.

* `softfma.rs` (a correctly rounded `fma` in integer arithmetic) and
  `selftest.rs`: written for DDNet-AI, derived from no other code (GPL-3.0
  like the rest of the repository).

The LGPL parts (the four ARM-derived files, `atan2.rs`, `hypot.rs`, and the
glibc-derived tables in `tables.rs`) are used under the GNU General Public
License. Section 3 of the GNU LGPL 2.1 says that a licensee "may opt to apply
the terms of the ordinary GNU General Public License instead of this License
to a given copy of the Library. To do this, you must alter all the notices
that refer to this License, so that they refer to the ordinary GNU General
Public License, version 2, instead of to this License (version 2.1 of the
Lesser General Public License). (If a newer version than version 2 of the
